#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125348} :activity/id #uuid "f4bd8552-cbfe-5cdf-9c63-a1215aabe6f0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050453 :topic/id #uuid "4a436386-bf41-55c7-b1d3-02adad8f3faf" :topic/math-academy-id 1907} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125348 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125762 :tutorial/content "Given a vector space $V$, any subset $H$ of $V,$ which is itself a vector space, is called a **subspace** of $V$. \n\nFortunately, we do not need to check all vector space axioms to determine whether a subset of some vector space is a subspace. Instead, we only need to check the **subspace criterion**, which states the following:\n\n*A subset $H$ of a vector space $V$ is a subspace of $V$ if and only if $H$ is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication. That is to say, both of the following conditions hold:*\n\n- *If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ lie in $H$, then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ also lies in $H$. Formally,*\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v} \\in H \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  \\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in H.\n$$\n\n- *If $\\mathbf{u}$ lies in $H$, then $k \\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ also lies in $H$ for any scalar  $k$. Formally,* \n\n$$\nk \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\,\\mathbf{u} \\in H \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  k\\cdot\\mathbf{u} \\in H.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_3[t]$ containing all polynomials of degree at most $3,$ along with the subset \n\n$$\nA = \\{ at^2 \\:|\\: a \\in \\mathbb{R} \\}.\n$$\n\nIs $A$ a subspace? Let's check the two subspace criterion conditions: \n\n- Suppose $p_1(t)=a_1 t^2$ and $p_2(t)=a_2 t^2$ are two polynomials from $A$. Summing the two polynomials, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}_{1}(t)+{p}_{2}(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{2})+({a}_{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={{({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})}_{⏟}}_{a\\in R}{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThus, $p_1(t)+p_2(t)$ is a polynomial of the form $at^2,$ which belongs to $A$.\n\n- If $p_1(t)=a_1 t^2 \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{p}_{1}(t) & =k({a}_{1}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={{(k{a}_{1})}_{⏟}}_{a\\in R}{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThus, $kp_1(t)$ is a polynomial of the form $at^2,$ which belongs to $A$.\n\nSince both conditions hold, we conclude that $A$ is a subspace $\\mathbb{R}_3[t].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4274a05d-d402-5dfc-a339-e5a6b5a8e5b0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3205 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c9024def-0495-5d67-911a-9b23824e8187" :step/math-academy-id 653353 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125595}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125349 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124301 :question/id #uuid "69da2e7c-7ede-5966-8ff9-13bca51c0420" :question/math-academy-id "e-4813"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048176}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493915}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "e3832943-8321-5094-b97b-66a6951219cb" :step/math-academy-id 653361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125524 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124401 :question/id #uuid "7fed39de-7304-52a0-a94d-e55703175d7d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8233"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380291}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "78c3b58c-ad85-58e8-8baf-c63bf9e544b1" :step/math-academy-id 653358 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125349}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125525 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124591 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124402 :question/id #uuid "05151ae0-102c-5ce8-8bc6-082e22aa77d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8234"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b1bb974-826a-590f-8d32-cdc868ca827c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051046}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481385}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Polynomial Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "f647c92d-413b-597e-a1c7-89a867776426" :step/math-academy-id 653355 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125524}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125595 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125858 :tutorial/content "Let's now look at an example of a subset of a vector space that is not a subspace.\n\nConsider the vector space $\\mathbb R_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2,$ and its subset \n\n$$\nB = \\big\\{ at+b \\: | \\: a,b  \\in\\mathbb{Z} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- $B$ is closed under polynomial addition. Indeed, suppose that $f(t)=a_1t+b_1$ and $g(t)=a_2t+b_2$ are two polynomials from $B.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}t+{b}_{1})+({a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})t+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $B$ since $a_1+a_2$ and $b_1+b_2$ are in $\\Bbb Z.$\n\n- $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication. As a counterexample, consider $f(t)=t$ and $k=0.5,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =0.5t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial from $B,$ because $0.5 \\notin \\Bbb Z.$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is not a subspace of $\\mathbb R_2[t].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "532d5411-c233-58a0-88e3-bd13bc8fcd1e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6193 :tutorial/title "Example of a Subset That Is Not a Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "fa129907-91b4-5bfa-8c65-22954ea25d25" :step/math-academy-id 653354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125525}}] :activity/title "Subspaces of Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124707}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481698} :activity/id #uuid "2d5bd931-ddfc-5c73-a933-79079c0774b8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050453 :topic/id #uuid "4a436386-bf41-55c7-b1d3-02adad8f3faf" :topic/math-academy-id 1907} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481698 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329724 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38cb7c6e-48d5-5719-9bc4-f341096b9869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450666} {:answer/id #uuid "acf32b73-2582-59f7-b014-ea9ea8e2b727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450667}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450667} :answer-field/id #uuid "deb7b878-00d8-573f-9c0a-f510c2c287a1" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451287} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e105135-fd28-5ebd-a7a8-7fa54306d756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450668} {:answer/id #uuid "45355d1b-48c6-519a-96e3-bbc8d43f590d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450669}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450669} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea3082d2-a25f-5513-8dfb-ccff48a59f9f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451288} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8d51c46-ebc6-5ee6-8799-43361686c56d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450670} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd955e0-f434-5f0d-9f8e-17ed74539b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450671}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450671} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2280d44-e6e2-5192-96b3-c76661eb0a1c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451289} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a3c8024-a57d-59ab-916d-48c342bfd6e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450672} {:answer/id #uuid "594cdb2e-1903-5d5b-a568-00aa3983c13d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450673}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450672} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a204198-fcd4-5ddf-9145-17e9ce86b916" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "32e0dd88-b99c-539d-9214-7424ba73f602" :question/math-academy-id "q-43775" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $E$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $\\alpha A$ where $A$ is some fixed $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\alpha \\in R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $E$ is closed under matrix addition\n- **II.** $E$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **III.** $E$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- **IV.** $E$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}={\\alpha }_{1}A$ and ${A}_{2}={\\alpha }_{2}A$ are two matrices from $E,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & ={\\alpha }_{1}A+{\\alpha }_{2}A=({\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{2})A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a matrix from $E.$ So, $E$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $B=\\alpha A$ is an element of $E$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kB & =k\\alpha A=(k\\alpha )A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a matrix from $E.$ So, $E$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $E$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "50493f64-ffe9-5fa7-9d7b-f97ed6fe8636" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481717}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481708 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378362 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9c8b6f7-a765-51f0-bdc1-0e20609fa1e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453226} {:answer/id #uuid "75058ef8-65b5-5339-9bcb-1c1175a9c585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453227}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453226} :answer-field/id #uuid "603a8296-6812-5aa0-acca-2e58013b4290" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454363} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aea5044e-ffdb-5981-9a0d-1e77af7bb0ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453228} {:answer/id #uuid "5e12f28f-5d7e-5ae4-9e88-d666b37d25f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453229}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453228} :answer-field/id #uuid "2086fc9f-b317-5fdc-9df7-fec846619a6b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454364} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73efe5e7-86d9-56bf-883f-fdf28547d599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453230} {:answer/id #uuid "a85494a0-51a8-59e6-96b9-be4532a22c50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453231}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453230} :answer-field/id #uuid "a819ffc8-0ac7-5428-aa18-b902a7881f2c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454365} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94acce7b-7200-5224-a551-0f52dea34c37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453232} {:answer/id #uuid "5cef8fe4-d2dc-5067-8776-abd7c9ca57d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453233}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453233} :answer-field/id #uuid "88425d2a-b4ad-54eb-89a3-3a3b986d9505" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ecd1a414-f091-5cfd-86f0-d0c82f6d9564" :question/math-academy-id "q-107147" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $K$ containing all nonconstant functions.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $K$ is closed under function addition\n- **II.** $K$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **III.** $K$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- **IV.** $K$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n\n*Hint: $f$ is a constant function if $f(x)=\\text{constant}$ for all $x\\in [0,1].$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, the functions $f(x)=x+2$ and $f(x)=-x-1$ are in $K$, however\n\n$$\n(f+g)(x)=1\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in [0,1]$ and hence $f+g$ is a constant function. \nSo, $K$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, the product of the scalar zero by any function is the constant function zero. So, $K$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $K$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""} :step/id #uuid "67adba61-524d-57c0-85ec-fefe501262e8"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb2d723d-c8b0-5e00-b156-69d0e470f0ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452001} {:answer/id #uuid "5b1f3a41-57bb-56b5-b700-a05f59bcdb05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452002}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452001} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaed178b-0ca9-53db-93f2-6c3fe7166a1e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452848} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d54602-5430-531a-a7c5-47d26991c196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452003} {:answer/id #uuid "99d476b2-00d2-510e-ab51-4d3a23898225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452004}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452003} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeee6e44-1273-539a-b514-732ed6cf2323" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452849} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63d370e5-07ba-54c6-84b2-d2152f843127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452005} {:answer/id #uuid "3b739ff6-ecb6-5572-a58c-6817071a785d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452006}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452005} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd8599eb-5b6c-5f72-9e7f-4c37a37b62cd" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452850} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aca4493b-8547-5286-83fb-deaf509091f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452007} {:answer/id #uuid "a167ab0d-34f6-5000-8bef-7f7a8ae3ed10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452008}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452008} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aa85b89-bf70-5661-b433-3d5ffe0aa95a" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0f62d508-5cf6-5993-b139-949d11db546e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94635" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $C$ containing all polynomials of degree exactly $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $C$ is closed under function addition\n- **II.** $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **III.** $C$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- **IV.** $C$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $g(x)=1-{x}^{3},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)+g(x) & ={x}^{3}+(1-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =({x}^{3}-{x}^{3})+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $C$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot {x}^{3}=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $C$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $C$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""} :step/id #uuid "72385174-3ed7-5267-8531-11fa251308c2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481708}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481717 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481385 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c159e3-322b-5045-961d-a4bb6de6bf28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480754} {:answer/id #uuid "0be6e3fd-a982-5d87-84bd-f85e41333d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480763} {:answer/id #uuid "62c9945e-be6a-5851-bfe4-b314ac1b71d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480920} {:answer/id #uuid "7f0c6a78-5937-5a8c-b3a3-be973c02f31d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480978} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d54a74-6c6a-5da4-9499-7d05f911b9ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481053}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481053} :answer-field/id #uuid "a24ecf47-9c4a-5174-a968-bb79cd6b4d3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "75f0d82a-dd66-5d66-8858-8e85c7e74ab7" :question/math-academy-id "q-94610" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3,$ and its subset $B=\\{a{t}^{3}+bt+1\\,|\\,a,b\\in R\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\nII. $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{3}[t]$\n\nIV. $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{3}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(t)=g(t)=t+1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =(t+1)+(t+1) \\\\  & =2t+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(t)=t+1$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =2(t+1)=2t+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""} :step/id #uuid "81941ef7-5939-5d4c-84c0-696db12e35ad" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "795fc022-fb5b-5f27-a5d8-aa4c2ad9dca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451961} {:answer/id #uuid "566a3a56-16f1-5b90-bea1-2f3ee57bb51e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451962}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451961} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0f4fd63-51fe-513d-929c-04e865c20a46" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452828} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39e01aaf-2818-586e-bb60-43cf7bd3de14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451963} {:answer/id #uuid "e083d6c2-56a6-577a-92e4-6ba01e342ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451964}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451963} :answer-field/id #uuid "5913234f-6a30-58d7-aabd-166bad7da71b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452829} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82e2ad3e-07c9-5fb2-9b3d-fe00002b1fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451965} {:answer/id #uuid "40263c7a-0627-5200-bf3d-c2777958353f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451965} :answer-field/id #uuid "963e43d6-331a-56e1-be82-da9c3690ac1f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452830} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18189e21-c99c-5d19-a3a8-4e8060b0797b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451967} {:answer/id #uuid "e6eb60f2-98a4-5b24-a94e-92f9324e2a78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451968}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451968} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bfdc7e3-3ad3-5cba-9513-6068b167a525" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "32515201-cdee-5802-875e-aaeaf04119b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94625" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $B={[\\begin{aligned}0 & a \\\\ 1-a & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a\\in R}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $B$ is closed under matrix addition\n- **II.** $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **III.** $B$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- **IV.** $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if ${A}_{1}={A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =2[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""} :step/id #uuid "f553b660-9aa6-5b79-a74c-70bbaa7d5027" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481710}}] :activity/title "Subspaces of Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481565}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494030} :activity/id #uuid "bba4c4cc-2793-5afd-91e2-7a137ae53cb5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050453 :topic/id #uuid "4a436386-bf41-55c7-b1d3-02adad8f3faf" :topic/math-academy-id 1907} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493915 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25ce7efb-3ad5-579b-809f-0dd2e31fe7e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493451} {:answer/id #uuid "87f00bc1-a093-5b90-a9c9-83e2777d88d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493604} {:answer/id #uuid "aca0bca7-0e7a-58b7-a3c4-843dab2110e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493662} {:answer/id #uuid "be1fae9a-76f7-5ae7-af20-eb786be794c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493703} {:answer/id #uuid "f14395c6-36b4-5cb6-8085-7a9935a781a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493780}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493451} :answer-field/id #uuid "28ce3e2a-2275-50a0-b25b-b9f3436f743c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3ec301c4-26d6-5f2a-9132-b21928f5ee67" :question/math-academy-id "q-94638" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,3]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,3]\\to R$ and its subset $N$ containing all functions that return a nonzero value at exactly one point in $[0,3].$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $N$ is closed under function addition\n\nII. $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $N$ is a subspace of $C[0,3]$\n\nIV. $N$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,3]$\n\n*Hint: Different functions from $N$ might give a nonzero value at different points in $[0,3].$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f({a}_{1})\\ne 0$ and $g({a}_{2})\\ne 0,$ where ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are distinct points in $[0,3],$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({a}_{1})+g({a}_{1}) & \\ne 0 \\\\ f({a}_{2})+g({a}_{2}) & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)+g(x)\\ne 0$ at two distinct points. So, $N$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(a)\\ne 0$ for some $a\\in [0,3],$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\n$$\n\n \nfor any $x\\in [0,3].$ So, $N$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $N$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""} :step/id #uuid "61b43f65-40c4-5ee2-a729-b625837e3d66" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494067}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494029 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b3a3727-45a2-5fb4-95ae-a91e8f4f3239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451353} {:answer/id #uuid "560e913c-ce8a-5991-9d57-1efe0344f54f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451354}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451354} :answer-field/id #uuid "7994875a-855a-584f-b0b1-e9adddf96e49" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452524} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f71e555b-f6a2-5bfa-a969-f07e226574ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451355} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe4cba6-6e86-5be1-8135-57a663228532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451356}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451356} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae639aa0-32cb-59c4-89fa-57a75c8e85c1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452525} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d934afb8-4872-512d-b557-15198d1a48a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451357} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f2982f-15e7-58c5-94f1-438da460594b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451358}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451358} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5a8d921-2a8a-515d-b4dc-86f366326881" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452526} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc6f1bb2-59b1-5487-b01f-c8947e4f8b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451359} {:answer/id #uuid "ee66f17e-227c-5e22-9f23-dbee97ac1f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451360}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451359} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9876e54-bdbd-59af-a25c-ae629c2446e5" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7ed6c488-d6f4-5c35-b1ec-2f3c2d68e465" :question/math-academy-id "q-43779" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[-1,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-1,1]\\to R$ and its subset $B={f\\in C[-1,1]\\,|\\,f(1)=f(-1)}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $B$ is closed under function addition\n- **II.** $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **III.** $B$ is a subspace of $C[-1,1]$\n- **IV.** $B$ is **not** a subspace of $C[-1,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(x),g(x)\\in B,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(1) & =f(1)+g(1) \\\\  & =f(-1)+g(-1) \\\\  & =(f+g)(-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $B$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(x)\\in B$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(1) & =kf(1) \\\\  & =kf(-1) \\\\  & =(kf)(-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $B$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "876842ab-2531-5a55-8715-683cc46f3d77" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494008}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494030 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125154 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8536f8a6-1bfc-5275-95ca-8a1fda0afe28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123525} {:answer/id #uuid "b659c777-4549-54ab-9f1f-bae85db6256b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123526} {:answer/id #uuid "5da722ba-7c66-52bf-862c-fc5ad59257b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123527} {:answer/id #uuid "64f059cc-f403-5120-b592-b5803d968b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123528} {:answer/id #uuid "79c277c4-a05b-5a16-ab06-0142128ff522" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123529}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186123528} :answer-field/id #uuid "856e0bc2-ed8c-5746-a814-6831d1a835e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "027d54ca-a5e1-5f8e-b0e8-aea8a3580062" :question/math-academy-id "q-43770" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a\\in R\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $C$ is closed under matrix addition\n\nII. $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $C$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nIV. $C$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $C,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a matrix from $C.$ So, $C$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $A=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ is an element of $C$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =k[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}ka & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a matrix from $C.$ So, $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $C$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "87d932f6-49ba-5c24-8d19-a6ad3580ecf9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494029}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494067 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "991f2f73-ac23-5191-ab80-7de9efee7e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452009} {:answer/id #uuid "2ead1013-7e82-50d2-9ea0-c2b7d5a04066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452010}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452010} :answer-field/id #uuid "506cf49e-9f52-5299-8ed3-6ff8e24478db" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452852} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fed6d2ec-aee8-5542-8372-ae559f20b93f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452011} {:answer/id #uuid "c05c0c3e-d50a-5489-b880-65368c86a869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452012}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452012} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b6ad786-f4e4-5537-b235-5beb1d76d0c8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452853} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e1613e4-3825-5163-955a-c508dc5a7424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452013} {:answer/id #uuid "f5a4d7ea-1e52-5939-b1ad-c82fa09897e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452014}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452014} :answer-field/id #uuid "860bab32-e063-5c7b-9daf-21735a207ce5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452854} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "902ecaea-e103-52f1-8446-e92ffd5c7e4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452015} {:answer/id #uuid "b5359c31-2887-5291-8d2f-9c2c74acea93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452016}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452015} :answer-field/id #uuid "063b6651-61e1-5617-8f54-44670f58aa5b" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "44096d11-ac2c-5302-8668-0431e5543e9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94642" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[-2,2]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-2,2]\\to R$ and its subset $E$ containing all even functions.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $E$ is closed under function addition\n- **II.** $E$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **III.** $E$ is a subspace of $C[-2,2]$\n- **IV.** $E$ is **not** a subspace of $C[-2,2]$\n\n*Hint: $f$ is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in [-2,2].$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(x),g(x)\\in E,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(-x) & =f(-x)+g(-x) \\\\  & =f(x)+g(x) \\\\  & =(f+g)(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $E$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(x)\\in E$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(-x) & =kf(-x) \\\\  & =kf(x) \\\\  & =(kf)(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $E$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $E$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ef5989bf-617a-54c7-bbc7-9e531c1f1c5b"}] :activity/title "Subspaces of Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493386}]}
